# Development cycle
- after each change:
  - run all unit tests
  - run all linters
  - check if local server is running (npm run dev), and if not then run it
    - IMPORTANT: Always check for existing dev servers first with `lsof -i :5173` or similar command
    - If a server is already running, use the existing one instead of starting a new one
    - If multiple servers are running, kill the extra ones with `pkill -f "node /path/to/project/node_modules/.bin/vite"`
    - Only start a new server with `npm run dev` if no server is currently running
  - use Playwright MCP to verify the change
- after finishing each set of changes, before preparing a commit:
  - make sure that it has unit test coverage
  - run all linters
  - run all unit tests
  - check if local server is running `npm run dev`, and if not then run it
  - use Playwright MCP to verify the changes
  - use `git status` and `git diff` to review the local changes again, REVIEW ALL THE LOCAL CHANGES
  - prepare git commit message using Gitmoji specified below
- after file name change or file removal
  - update/remove all references to the file in the code
  - update/remove all imports and exports
  - update/remove all references to the file in the tests
  - update/remove all references to the file in the documentation

# Playwright MCP flow

- before running Playwright MCP:
  - make sure that local server is running `npm run dev`, and if not then run it
  - open .env file and read API_KEY value
  - set API_KEY value in the application's configuration UI (go to Config page and set Snipe Ballistics API Key)
- use Playwright MCP to verify the changes and finish each test by:
  - pressing "Calculate" button on the Calculator page
  - checking if the solution grid contains results
  - if the grid doesn't contain results, check if the error message is visible
  - see the underlaying API response in the Network tab of the browser's developer tools
  - **Note**: CORS errors in console are expected and do not indicate test failure

# API Request Debugging and CORS Handling

- **API Request Preview**: Use configurable logging to preview API request payloads:
  - Set `VITE_LOG_API_REQUESTS=true` in environment variables for global logging
  - Or call `calculateBallistics(true)` for specific request logging
  - Logs show complete request payload including atmosphere, shot, and preference data
  - This replaces the need to view browser network tab for payload inspection
- **CORS Issues**: CORS errors when testing from localhost are expected and normal:
  - The API endpoint `https://api.calculator.snipe.technology` doesn't allow localhost origins
  - CORS errors do NOT indicate problems with state management or payload construction
  - The request payload is correctly built and sent despite CORS blocking the response
  - Use the configurable logging to verify payload correctness instead of relying on successful API responses

# Gitmoji Commit Message Specification

This paragraph defines the standard format for commit messages in this project using gitmojis.
Each commit message should start with an appropriate gitmoji code based on the type of change. Use description from Gitmoji Reference below to match a proper gitmoji. The commit message can start with more than one gitmoji. Note that there's a difference between fixing a functional bug :bug: and fixing a linter warning :rotating_light:. The :rotating_light: gitmoji should be used for linter warnings.

## Commit format
Here's a commit format:

```
<intention> [scope?][:?] <message>
```

- `intention`: An emoji from the list.
- `scope`: An optional string that adds contextual information for the scope of the change.
- `message`: A brief explanation of the change.

